1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,690
Automatic captions can turn a medical term into a plausible word.

2
00:00:04,690 --> 00:00:09,450
MedCue helps education editors compare that text with the recording.

3
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:12,810
Open the subtitles and matching audio.

4
00:00:12,810 --> 00:00:18,470
Listen to a cue, edit against the original, then review and continue.

5
00:00:18,470 --> 00:00:21,180
The editor controls every change.

6
00:00:21,430 --> 00:00:23,110
Here is the working app.

7
00:00:23,110 --> 00:00:28,970
This example loads fourteen actual automatic captions and a matching public National

8
00:00:28,970 --> 00:00:31,060
Library of Medicine recording.

9
00:00:31,310 --> 00:00:38,640
Cue four contains the word mersa. Its original text and nearby context remain visible.

10
00:00:38,890 --> 00:00:46,410
Source recording: National Library of Medicine. Selected cue with context.

11
00:00:46,660 --> 00:00:51,390
The workspace separates the original evidence from the final subtitle.

12
00:00:51,390 --> 00:00:55,990
Listening guides the correction, while the original stays unchanged.

13
00:00:56,240 --> 00:00:59,490
After a refresh, the selected cue returns.

14
00:00:59,490 --> 00:01:05,390
Restore example audio reattaches the recording without replacing the review.

15
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:09,160
After listening, the editor corrects the wording.

16
00:01:09,160 --> 00:01:15,060
This is a prepared demonstration, not an automatic correction or an accuracy test.

17
00:01:15,310 --> 00:01:21,060
Review and next records the decision and selects the next cue in one action.

18
00:01:21,310 --> 00:01:27,580
Undo restores the decision and returns to its cue, so a mistaken action is recoverable.

19
00:01:27,830 --> 00:01:34,850
Only one cue has been reviewed. Export keeps the remaining thirteen visible as pending.

20
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:39,850
Every imported cue and its original timing will be included.

21
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:46,850
This inspection overlay shows the actual downloaded text, not a replacement exporter.

22
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:50,800
The words change while the timestamps stay intact.

23
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:55,460
A separate project file preserves review decisions and history.

24
00:01:55,710 --> 00:02:02,040
The parser, waveform playback, glossary rules and draft storage run in the browser.

25
00:02:02,040 --> 00:02:08,680
Audio stays on the device. The public site supports this editing loop without a server.

26
00:02:08,930 --> 00:02:15,250
The optional server edition sends only the selected cue and glossary after an explicit

27
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:19,440
 click. A guarded Node API contacts the configured model.

28
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:22,740
The public Pages site does not run inference.

29
00:02:22,990 --> 00:02:29,400
Earlier selected Gemini checks produced useful leads and a misplaced warning on a correct

30
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:35,890
term. These are recorded responses. Suggestions are unverified and never apply themselves.

31
00:02:36,140 --> 00:02:42,240
Developer checks preserved text and timestamps across two hundred fifty seven actual

32
00:02:42,240 --> 00:02:48,640
 automatic caption cues. Twenty two core and API checks and fourteen browser tests pass.

33
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:53,070
Human time savings and medical correctness remain unmeasured.

34
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:56,350
Try the real recording at the public site.

35
00:02:56,350 --> 00:03:02,570
The source repository includes server setup, evaluation evidence and the limits of the

36
00:03:02,570 --> 00:03:03,290
prototype.
